WEBVTT

NOTE Sentence timings are approximate, not word-aligned.

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.167
Here are two tasks that one small model can solve
together.

2
00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:08.077
First, watch its shared weights move as it learns task
A.

3
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.748
Now train on B alone, and A's prediction gets worse.

4
00:00:12.748 --> 00:00:15.768
Instead, replay an example from task A.

5
00:00:15.768 --> 00:00:19.021
In this toy, that helps retain both tasks.

6
00:00:19.021 --> 00:00:25.915
Both paths use equal example counts, but replay needs
memory and sees fewer new examples.

7
00:00:26.587 --> 00:00:29.115
Now consider which memory to replay.

8
00:00:29.115 --> 00:00:35.645
The hardest example right now is different from the one
most harmed by a hypothetical update.

9
00:00:35.645 --> 00:00:40.069
That update is only used to score the memories, then
discarded.

10
00:00:40.753 --> 00:00:44.894
These examples explain forgetting and two selection
scores.

11
00:00:44.894 --> 00:00:47.701
They don't establish a winning selector.

12
00:00:47.701 --> 00:00:53.105
The repository's controlled studies include inconclusive
and adverse results.
